# ------------------------------------------------------------------ abstract
d.heading("Abstract")
f1, f2_ = R["sem"], R["cfa"]
d.para(f"**Purpose.** Individual investors increasingly consult generative AI (GenAI) for stock analysis, yet fluent but unverified answers may inflate perceived competence without raising actual skill. We test whether GenAI use lowers investment decision quality through an overconfidence (“competence illusion”) channel and an uncritical-reliance channel, and whether a verification channel and two competences—financial knowledge and AI literacy—offset these effects. "
       f"**Design.** Covariance-based structural equation modelling with fully latent variables (no item composites), latent interactions, and bootstrap conditional-indirect effects (B = {R['boot_n']}), on a questionnaire-plus-knowledge-test design. "
       f"**Findings.** GenAI use is associated with higher perceived competence (β = {m(PA['PCA~GUI']['beta'])}) and a larger calibration gap between predicted and actual knowledge-test scores (β = {m(PA['CGz~GUI']['beta'])}), and with more uncritical reliance (β = {m(PA['UR~GUI']['beta'])}) but also more verification (β = {m(PA['VER~GUI']['beta'])}). Illusion and reliance transmit negative indirect effects on decision quality, verification a positive one, so the net indirect effect is {'distinguishable from zero' if sig('total_ind_mean') else 'statistically indistinguishable from zero'} (indirect = {ci('total_ind_mean')}). Financial knowledge weakens the use→illusion links, and AI literacy weakens the use→reliance link and strengthens the use→verification link. "
       f"**Originality.** The study separates illusion, reliance and verification in one model and shows that the decision-quality cost of GenAI depends on how it is used and by whom. "
       f"**Data note.** The data analysed here are synthetic, generated to demonstrate the design and code; they are not empirical evidence.", indent=False)
d.para("**Keywords:** generative artificial intelligence; overconfidence; automation bias; financial literacy; AI literacy; investor decision quality; structural equation modelling", indent=False)
d.para("**JEL:** G11, G41, D91, O33", indent=False)
d.heading("Transparency statement on the data", 2)
d.para(f"The sample (N = {n}) is **simulated**. It was generated in Python from a pre-specified data-generating process (seed 31415) with realistic Likert thresholds, cross-loadings, correlated residuals and a weak method factor, so that the pipeline, the models and the reporting could be developed and tested before fieldwork. All coefficients, p values and confidence intervals reported below are properties of this synthetic sample and **must not be cited as empirical findings**. The manuscript, questionnaire, code and data are released together so that the same script can be run on real data (set DATA_PATH). Hypotheses are stated ex ante and are supported in the synthetic data by construction; with real data, partial support or non-support is the expected and informative outcome.", indent=False)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1 intro
d.heading("1. Introduction")
d.para("Since late 2022, general-purpose large language models have become a routine research assistant for retail investors: they summarise filings, explain ratios and propose trades in seconds. Early evidence suggests that such models can extract signal from financial text (Lopez-Lira & Tang, 2023), but the same systems can state falsehoods fluently (Ji et al., 2023), and investors have long been shown to trade too much and earn too little when they overestimate their own ability (Barber & Odean, 2000, 2001; Glaser & Weber, 2007; Grinblatt & Keloharju, 2009). The behavioural question is therefore not whether GenAI is accurate on average, but what it does to the //investor's// judgement: does it leave the user better informed, or merely more confident?")
d.para("Two literatures speak to this question but rarely meet. Research on overconfidence and the Dunning–Kruger pattern shows that people with little knowledge poorly recognise the limits of that knowledge (Kruger & Dunning, 1999; Ehrlinger et al., 2008) and that miscalibration takes distinct forms—overestimation, over-placement and over-precision (Moore & Healy, 2008). Research on human–automation interaction shows that decision aids invite complacency and automation bias—accepting an aid's output without adequate checking (Parasuraman & Manzey, 2010; Skitka et al., 1999; Goddard et al., 2012)—and that appropriate reliance requires trust calibrated to reliability (Lee & See, 2004; Hoff & Bashir, 2015). Evidence on algorithm aversion and appreciation shows that people sometimes over- and sometimes under-weight algorithmic advice (Dietvorst et al., 2015; Logg et al., 2019), and robo-advisory work documents both benefits and pitfalls for household finance (D'Acunto et al., 2019). Recent commentary warns that AI can create //illusions of understanding// (Messeri & Crockett, 2024). What is missing, to our knowledge, is an investor-level model that places an //illusion// channel, an //uncritical-reliance// channel and a //verification// channel side by side, links them to decision quality, and asks which competences make GenAI use safe.")
d.para("We contribute in three ways. First, we separate three behavioural consequences of GenAI use that are usually conflated: inflated perceived competence (measured both by self-report and by a behavioural calibration gap), uncritical reliance, and verification. Second, we show how the net association between use and decision quality arises as the sum of opposing indirect effects, which explains why a simple use→quality regression can show little (the “suppression” case). Third, we test two boundary conditions with direct managerial and regulatory relevance: objective financial knowledge, which should reduce the inflation of confidence, and AI literacy, which should reduce blind reliance and raise checking. We also provide an openly documented instrument, with each scale classified as adapted or researcher-made, to support replication.")
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2 theory
d.heading("2. Theory and hypotheses")
d.heading("2.1 GenAI use and the competence illusion", 2)
d.para("Fluent, well-structured explanations generate a feeling of understanding that need not reflect competence. When an investor receives a polished analysis, the cognitive effort of producing it is outsourced, but the sense of mastery is retained (Messeri & Crockett, 2024). Overconfidence in finance is typically defined as miscalibrated beliefs about one's own ability or the precision of one's forecasts (Moore & Healy, 2008; Glaser & Weber, 2007). We therefore expect heavier GenAI users to feel more competent //with// AI (perceived competence, PCA) and to overestimate their objective knowledge relative to a test (calibration gap, CG).")
d.para("**H1.** GenAI use is positively related to perceived competence with AI.  **H2.** GenAI use is positively related to the calibration gap (overestimation).")
d.heading("2.2 Reliance, verification and decision quality", 2)
d.para("Automation-bias research defines two error types—omission (failing to act because the aid is silent) and commission (following incorrect advice)—and attributes them to cognitive-miser heuristics and diffused responsibility (Skitka et al., 1999; Parasuraman & Manzey, 2010; Goddard et al., 2012). In investing, the analogue is accepting an AI answer about a ratio, an audit opinion or a price trend without cross-checking. We define uncritical reliance (UR) as the tendency to accept AI output without independent checks, and verification (VER) as the behavioural counterpart of calibrated reliance: comparing AI claims with filings, independent sources and own calculations (Lee & See, 2004; Hoff & Bashir, 2015). Heavier use should raise both, because use creates both opportunity and exposure; but only verification should protect the quality of decisions.")
d.para("**H3.** GenAI use is positively related to uncritical reliance.  **H4.** GenAI use is positively related to verification (competing mechanism).")
d.para("Decision quality (IDQ) reflects documented analysis, satisfaction with outcomes, goal alignment and low regret (Parker & Fischhoff, 2005; Bruine de Bruin et al., 2007). Overconfident investors trade more and perform worse (Barber & Odean, 2000), and reliance on incorrect advice degrades outcomes, whereas checking limits errors.")
d.para("**H5.** Perceived competence with AI is negatively related to decision quality.  **H6.** The calibration gap is negatively related to decision quality.  **H7.** Uncritical reliance is negatively related to decision quality.  **H8.** Verification is positively related to decision quality.")
d.para("**H9 (mediation).** GenAI use has negative indirect effects on decision quality through perceived competence, the calibration gap and uncritical reliance, and a positive indirect effect through verification; the net indirect effect is the sum of opposing paths.")
d.heading("2.3 Boundary conditions: financial knowledge and AI literacy", 2)
d.para("Knowledge provides the standard against which an AI answer can be judged, and the metacognitive skill to see where it is lacking. Higher objective financial and accounting knowledge should therefore weaken the translation of use into inflated confidence (Kruger & Dunning, 1999; Lusardi & Mitchell, 2014; Van Rooij et al., 2011). AI literacy—understanding that GenAI can hallucinate, is trained on dated data, and must be evaluated (Long & Magerko, 2020; Ng et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2023)—should reduce blind acceptance and increase checking, because the user knows //when// not to rely.")
d.para("**H10.** Financial knowledge weakens the relation of GenAI use to (a) perceived competence and (b) the calibration gap.  **H11.** AI literacy (a) weakens the relation of use to uncritical reliance and (b) strengthens its relation to verification.  Moderated-mediation indices (Hayes, 2015) test whether the conditional indirect effects differ across moderator levels.")
FIG = os.path.join(OUT, "output", "fig_p3_model.png"); sem_fig(R, FIG)
d.figure(FIG, "Figure 1. Estimated structural model (standardized coefficients). Latent variables are ellipses; observed variables are rectangles; dashed red paths are interactions.")
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3 method
d.heading("3. Data and method")
d.heading("3.1 Design and sample (synthetic)", 2)
dm = R["demo"]
d.para(f"The planned study is a cross-sectional online survey of active individual stock investors with an embedded knowledge test. The synthetic sample has N = {n}; {dm['male']:.0f}% male, mean age {dm['age_m']:.1f} years (SD = {dm['age_sd']:.1f}), mean trading experience {dm['exp_m']:.1f} years (SD = {dm['exp_sd']:.1f}), and {dm['users_pct']:.0f}% reporting at least occasional GenAI use for investing (GUI1 ≥ 3). Because the model has {R['n_params_sem']} free parameters, the ratio of observations to parameters is {n/R['n_params_sem']:.1f}:1; we therefore rely on a power analysis for covariance-structure models (MacCallum et al., 1996), which gives power above .99 to reject close fit (RMSEA = .05) against RMSEA = .08 at this N and these degrees of freedom, and we report DWLS and trimmed-sample checks. For real data, a target of N ≥ 400 with at least 40% regular users is recommended so that the exogenous construct keeps its variance.")
d.heading("3.2 Measures", 2)
d.para("Table 1 lists each scale, its origin and its principal sources; the full item wording, scoring and validation protocol are in the accompanying questionnaire (Appendix A and the separate questionnaire file). Latent variables are indicated by their own items in the structural model; no summed or averaged composites enter any model.", indent=False)
rows = [(v["code"], v["name"], str(len(v["items"])), v["cls"]) for v in P.VARS]
d.table("Table 1. Measures and origin of the scales", ["Code", "Construct", "Items", "Origin"], rows, widths=[700, 4400, 700, 3500])
d.para("//Calibration gap.// After the eight-item knowledge test, respondents predict how many items they answered correctly (C1). The gap is CG = C1 − actual score (positive = overestimation), the “overestimation” form of miscalibration in the taxonomy of Moore & Healy (2008). It is standardized (CGz) and enters the model as an observed mediator. //Financial knowledge// is the standardized test score (FKz; KR-20 = " + f"{R['fk']['kr20']:.2f}" + "); a latent single-factor treatment is unnecessary because items are binary and the score is the test's intended output. //Interactions// are latent: GUI×AIL and GUI×FKz are represented by matched-pair product indicators (Marsh et al., 2004) with residual-centred indicators as a robustness check (Little et al., 2006).")
d.heading("3.3 Analytic strategy", 2)
d.para("Models were estimated by maximum likelihood in semopy (Igolkina & Meshcheryakov, 2020) in Python. Fit is judged jointly: CFI/TLI ≥ .95, RMSEA ≤ .06, SRMR ≤ .08 (Hu & Bentler, 1999), with the χ² test reported but not used alone because it over-rejects at this size. Convergent and discriminant validity use CR, AVE (Fornell & Larcker, 1981) and HTMT (Henseler et al., 2015). Common-method bias is probed by Harman's single-factor test, a one-factor CFA and a common-latent-factor model (Podsakoff et al., 2003). Indirect and conditional indirect effects use 2,000 nonparametric bootstrap draws with percentile intervals (Preacher & Hayes, 2008), evaluated at −1, 0 and +1 SD of the moderator, and indices of moderated mediation follow Hayes (2015). R² for latent outcomes is 1 − the standardized residual variance; the effect size of an interaction is f² from the R² change. Five competing structures (M1–M5) are compared by AIC/BIC and fit.")
