d.heading("4.7 Behavioural criterion validity", 2)
v = R["vignette"]
d.para(f"As an external check that does not share response format with the scales, respondents judged four AI statements about valuation and accounting, two of them wrong (Appendix A, V1–V4). The number of wrong statements accepted was predicted positively by uncritical reliance ({bz('VIGERR~UR', v)}) and negatively by verification ({bz('VIGERR~VER', v)}), controlling for use ({bz('VIGERR~GUI', v)}). This shows that the self-reported reliance and verification factors map onto observable acceptance of incorrect advice.")
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5 discussion
d.heading("5. Discussion")
d.para("The synthetic results illustrate the pattern the theory predicts and the design is built to detect: GenAI use raises feelings of competence and overestimation, and raises uncritical reliance, each of which is linked to poorer decision quality; at the same time heavier users also verify more, and verification is the strongest positive correlate of quality. The net association between use and quality is therefore small, which would be mistaken for “no effect” in a simple regression but hides two opposing behavioural channels. If the pattern replicates on real data, it implies that the question “does GenAI help investors?” should be replaced by “which uses, by whom, with which checking habits?”")
d.para("Both competences operate as boundary conditions in the predicted directions. Objective knowledge buffers the inflation of confidence—consistent with the metacognitive account in which the same knowledge that produces competence also allows its limits to be recognised (Kruger & Dunning, 1999; Ehrlinger et al., 2008)—while AI literacy changes the //kind// of reliance rather than its quantity: literate users do not use AI less, they check it more. This is consistent with calibrated-trust accounts (Lee & See, 2004) and suggests that training that targets verification routines may matter more than warnings alone.")
d.para("//Practical implications.// For investor-education bodies, brokers and regulators, the findings (if replicated) imply (i) pairing GenAI features in investment apps with prompts to verify against official filings, (ii) offering calibration feedback (predict-then-test exercises), and (iii) targeting AI literacy content at heavy users with low objective knowledge, who show the largest conditional illusion effect. For auditors and accounting educators, items on audit opinions, cash-flow versus profit and ratio calculation can serve as a diagnostic of knowledge against which confidence is benchmarked.")
d.heading("6. Limitations and future research")
d.para("First and foremost, the data are synthetic; the coefficients demonstrate the pipeline, not a finding. Second, with real data the design remains cross-sectional and self-reported, so causal ordering (use → illusion) cannot be established; overconfident investors may also self-select into heavy use. Panel data, or an experiment that randomises exposure to correct versus incorrect AI advice and AI-label versus analyst-label (Dietvorst et al., 2015; Logg et al., 2019), would identify causal effects. Third, the calibration gap is based on an eight-item test and a single prediction; richer tests, over-placement (percentile rank) and over-precision (confidence intervals) should be added. Fourth, decision quality is self-reported; trading records (turnover, returns, disposition effect) would give behavioural outcomes. Fifth, the interaction effects are small, and latent-interaction estimates are sensitive to the product-indicator strategy; we therefore report two strategies. Sixth, scales are new or adapted and need the validation protocol (content validity, pilot, CFA, invariance across countries and languages) before inferential use. Finally, generalisability to markets with different disclosure regimes and AI access remains open.")
d.heading("7. Conclusion")
d.para("Generative AI can make investors feel more competent faster than it makes them more competent. The design proposed here separates illusion, uncritical reliance and verification, and tests the two competences—objective knowledge and AI literacy—that should make GenAI use safe. The synthetic demonstration shows the model is identified, estimable and interpretable; the decisive test is a real, pre-registered survey.")
d.heading("Declarations")
d.para("**Data and code availability.** Synthetic dataset (CSV/XLSX), the Python script (paper3_analysis.py, with simlib.py) and the Colab notebook are provided with this manuscript. **Ethics.** For real data collection, ethics approval and informed consent are required; the present synthetic data involve no human participants. **Funding / conflicts of interest.** [to be completed]. **AI-assistance disclosure.** AI tools were used to draft code and text; the authors are responsible for the content and must verify all references and DOIs against Crossref before submission.", indent=False)
d.heading("References")
